============================================================
MVIEW DEVELOPMENT LOG
day 6
 PaintBox Investigation and Rendering Architecture
============================================================

OBJECTIVE
---------
Connect the renderer to a drawing surface and understand how
Lazarus performs painting.

------------------------------------------------------------
1. Renderer constructor
------------------------------------------------------------

The renderer constructor had previously been changed to

    constructor Create(APaintBox: TPaintBox;
                       AConfig: TConfig);

This complicated object construction because TMView was not
yet ready to provide these dependencies.

Decision:
    Revert to

        constructor Create;

until the rendering pipeline is established.

Reason:
Keep construction simple while the architecture evolves.

------------------------------------------------------------
2. Introducing SetPaintBox()
------------------------------------------------------------

Instead of constructor injection, a setter was introduced.

TMView

    procedure SetPaintBox(APaintBox: TPaintBox);

TRenderer

    procedure SetPaintBox(APaintBox: TPaintBox);

This allows the form to supply the PaintBox after all controls
have been created.

Architecture

    TMainForm
        │
        ▼
    TMView.SetPaintBox()
        │
        ▼
    TRenderer.SetPaintBox()

------------------------------------------------------------
3. Ownership reminder
------------------------------------------------------------

TMView owns the renderer.

Therefore

    TMView forwards the PaintBox

instead of

    Renderer attempting to configure itself.

Responsibility remains:

    Form
        ↓
    TMView
        ↓
    Renderer

------------------------------------------------------------
4. Public vs Private
------------------------------------------------------------

Compiler error

    identifier idents no member "SetPaintBox"

Cause

    SetPaintBox had been placed in the private section.

Solution

    Move SetPaintBox into the public section.

------------------------------------------------------------
5. PaintBox added to MainForm
------------------------------------------------------------

A PaintBox component was added.

The FormShow event performs

    FMView.SetPaintBox(PaintBox1);
    FMView.OpenMedia(...);

This successfully connected the rendering pipeline.

------------------------------------------------------------
6. Verifying execution flow
------------------------------------------------------------

ShowMessage debugging confirmed

    FormShow
        ↓
    OpenMedia
        ↓
    Renderer.Render()

All methods execute correctly.

------------------------------------------------------------
7. PaintBox investigation
------------------------------------------------------------

Initial observation

    Rendering appeared to do nothing.

Experiment

    PaintBox.OnPaint

was implemented to fill the client area red.

Result

    PaintBox displayed correctly.

Conclusions

✓ PaintBox exists.

✓ PaintBox is visible.

✓ Canvas works.

✓ OnPaint fires correctly.

The issue is therefore NOT the PaintBox.

------------------------------------------------------------
8. Understanding the paint model
------------------------------------------------------------

Important discovery

Calling

    Canvas.FillRect(...)

directly from Render()

is not the correct rendering model.

Reason

Windows owns repainting.

A PaintBox only paints when Windows sends a paint message.

Drawing outside that cycle is temporary and may disappear.

------------------------------------------------------------
9. Invalidate()
------------------------------------------------------------

Learned the purpose of

    PaintBox.Invalidate;

Invalidate does NOT paint.

Instead it tells Windows

    "This control needs repainting."

Sequence

    Invalidate()
          ↓
    Windows posts paint message
          ↓
    OnPaint
          ↓
    Drawing occurs

------------------------------------------------------------
10. Future renderer design
------------------------------------------------------------

Current approach

    OpenMedia()
        ↓
    Render()

Future approach

    OpenMedia()
        ↓
    Renderer stores current media
        ↓
    Invalidate()

Later

    PaintBox.OnPaint
        ↓
    Renderer.Paint(Canvas)

This separates

"What should be displayed?"

from

"How should it be drawn?"

------------------------------------------------------------
11. PaintBox discussion
------------------------------------------------------------

Question

    Is PaintBox permanent?

Answer

Probably not.

PaintBox has served as an educational stepping stone.

Long-term direction is likely

    TImageView

(a custom control)

which will own

    painting
    zooming
    panning
    resize handling
    mouse interaction
    overlays
    double buffering

Architecture may become

    TMainForm
         │
         ▼
    TImageView
         │
         ▼
    TRenderer

instead of exposing PaintBox directly.

------------------------------------------------------------
12. Decision
------------------------------------------------------------

Do not continue investing effort into PaintBox-specific code.

Undo temporary debugging code.

Keep only the architectural improvements.

Next session:

Design the custom TImageView control and migrate the renderer
to use it as the application's drawing surface.

